      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224492",
      ":question/problem": "A particular intersection has an average accident rate of $12$ per year. The local authority decided to introduce speed cameras to reduce the accident rate. They conducted a hypothesis test at the $5%$ significance level to test whether the accident rate has subsequently decreased.\n\nA total of $6$ accidents occurred at the intersection one year after the cameras were installed. The local authority compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X\u223cPo(12)$.\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0203$ | $0.0458$ | $0.0895$ | $0.1550$ | $0.2424$ | $0.3472$ | $0.4616$ | $0.5760$ |\n\nBy assuming that the accidents occur independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of accidents in a randomly selected $12$-month period, then the test statistic is\n$X \\le 12$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $5%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the accident rate has reduced since the cameras were installed."
    },
