      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224970",
      ":question/problem": "A coffee shop had an average customer arrival rate of $30$ per hour. The shop owner decided to increase prices in an attempt to make the shop more profitable but he suspects this will decrease the number of customers. He conducted a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to test whether the customer arrival rate has subsequently reduced.\n\nA total of $22$ customers arrived at the coffee shop within the randomly chosen $60$-minute period the first day after the prices were increased. The owner compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X\u223cPo(30)$.\n\n| $x$ | $16$ | $17$ | $18$ | $19$ | $20$ | $21$ | $22$ | $23$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.0039$ | $0.0073$ | $0.0129$ | $0.0219$ | $0.0353$ | $0.0544$ | $0.0806$ | $0.1146$ |\n\nBy assuming that the customer arrivals occur independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of customers arriving in a randomly selected $60$-minute period, then the test statistic is\n$X = 30$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the customer arrival rate has reduced since the prices were increased."
    }
